ARM9 DMA bypasses the TCMs, as on hardware.

The ARM9's DMA controller sits on the system bus; ITCM and DTCM are private to
the ARM946E-S core and invisible to it (melonDS: DMA::Run9 goes through
NDS::ARM9Read/Write, which never consult the TCMs). nds_dma_run moved units
through bus_read_u32/bus_write_u32 - the CPU's own path, which maps both - so a
transfer aimed at a TCM window wrote the TCM.

LSW2 hits it in four-player Game Settings: a VBlank VRAM upload with a
null-based destination (0x00010400, an ITCM mirror) zeroed 2.5 KB of ITCM code
(0x01FF8400-0x01FF8DFF) and the next call into it ran garbage. On hardware the
same DMA writes nothing. Now: an ARM9 DMA address in the ITCM window reads 0 and
drops writes; one under the DTCM window reaches main RAM through the adjacent
4 MB mirror. ARM7 DMA is unchanged.

